{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-3077",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{8a{b}^{3}}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{9b} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{8a{b}^{3}\\cdot 9b} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{72a{b}^{4}} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{36{b}^{4}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{(6{b}^{2})^{2}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{(6{b}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot |6{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot |6|\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot 6\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 6\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 36\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\sqrt{2a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
